            <Notes>
                <Note>
                    <NoteId>0</NoteId>
                    <NoteColor>0xf9ec72ff</NoteColor>
                    <NoteContent>The main problem is that both classes are conceptually overlapping: students may be employees as well. In such cases two unrelated objects must be maintained separately: a clear threat to integrity.</NoteContent>
                    <NotePosition>
                        <XPosition>294.0</XPosition>
                        <YPosition>79.0</YPosition>
                    </NotePosition>
                </Note>
                <Note>
                    <NoteId>1</NoteId>
                    <NoteColor>0xf9ec72ff</NoteColor>
                    <NoteContent>Instances of STUDENT_GEN and EMPLOYEE_GEN show how easy model integrity may be violated here. There is no means to know we are referring to the same person.</NoteContent>
                    <NotePosition>
                        <XPosition>343.0</XPosition>
                        <YPosition>503.0</YPosition>
                    </NotePosition>
                </Note>
                <Note>
                    <NoteId>2</NoteId>
                    <NoteColor>0xf9ec72ff</NoteColor>
                    <NoteContent>Delegation as a solution: only one PERSON_DEL (role filler) object is needed where we can centrally manage the person Karolin Gerber. For each role she fulfills, a separate object is required. If a role object cannot handle an incoming request, it is delegated to the role-filler object.</NoteContent>
                    <NotePosition>
                        <XPosition>1472.0</XPosition>
                        <YPosition>265.0</YPosition>
                    </NotePosition>
                </Note>
            </Notes>
